WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.160
Each of those words conjures up
a different picture.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.880
None of them tell you
exactly who someone is.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.040
My name
is Stephen Akpabio-Klementowski.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.360
I’m a PhD candidate

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.920
and I’m a lecturer in criminology
at The Open University.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.800
I work with students in prisons.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.080
I love what I do.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.880
It feels deeply personal to me,

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.640
because I used to be a prisoner too.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.800
All of these labels have been mine.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.400
So growing up, life was tough.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:41.480
My father died in a car crash
when I was a teenager.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.160
And that hit me really hard.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.000
I didn’t see the world
as a meritocracy.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.760
I had to grab what I could.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.680
But being sent to prison
for dealing drugs was a shock.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:54.840
I was sentenced to 16 years.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.200
For the first three months inside,

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:58.880
I didn’t speak to anyone.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.280
Eventually, I started working
in the kitchens.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.000
And as people got to know me,

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.040
I was eventually assessed
for my educational potential

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:12.960
and encouraged after that assessment
to enroll at the Open University.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.760
But the most difficult barrier

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.640
was actually inside of me.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.680
I’d left school with no
qualifications. Nothing.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.760
I was scared of my future

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.600
and I decided to try.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.440
My day job working in the kitchens
and on the servery

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:29.800
meant that I had to study at night.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.120
So I had to study on the toilet,
while my cellmate snored.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.440
So when I finished my first module,

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.600
it gave me hope, and it gave me
something I could focus on.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:41.520
There was no going back now.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:46.680
Other prisoners and guards kept
asking me why I was wasting my time -

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.080
studying wouldn’t matter
with my criminal record.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:51.760
I felt I was changing.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:55.960
I discovered I loved learning. And
that was enough to keep me going.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.520
I served eight years
of my 16-year sentence.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:02.800
By the time I left prison,
I had completed my first degree.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.840
I had also completed two further
degrees at Masters level.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.920
So, after I was released, I got a job
working with students in prisons -

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.280
not in spite of who I was,
but because of it.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.400
It’s hard to describe how I felt

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.920
the first time I went back
to prison as a lecturer,

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.840
and the governor came down,
and shook my hand.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:24.520
What I want people to know

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.400
is that I’m not
different or special -

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.880
anybody can do this.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.600
Almost half of all prisoners
have left school

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.720
without achieving any
formal qualification.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.480
I know how that feels. And it had
a massive impact on my confidence.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.160
But that does not mean that
you’re not able to learn

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:46.920
Everyone has the potential
and the power to change.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.360
I’ve seen it. And I’ve lived it.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.720
It was Winston Churchill who said:

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:56.960
“There is treasure, if you can only
find it, in the heart of every man.”

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.000
What do we want from our prisons?

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.280
Is the primary goal
of prison to punish,

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.720
or to help find a different path?

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.560
The policy isn’t coherent, you see.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.920
Research shows that education
does reduce reoffending.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.720
It allows former prisoners
to make different choices.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.720
So when I sit with prisoners,
I say to them:

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:24.240
“I was in your shoes. But I am
now released, on the outside.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:28.920
I’ve got a good job. I’ve got
a good life. I’m with my family.”

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.480
Had I met somebody like me
when I was younger,

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.520
things might have been
very different.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:38.520
No-one believed in me.
I didn’t believe in myself either.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.400
It’s taken two decades to get here.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:43.160
And this is only the beginning. 

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.720
The word “prisoner” is just a label.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.920
I found freedom within my own mind.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.120
You need to remember that
you have the capacity to learn.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.760
You have the capacity
to change your life.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:58.520
There is treasure within
each and every one of us.

